.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt SYSEXTCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sysextctl
.Nd manage kernel extension policy and boot activation
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm status | list | config | reload | restore
.Nm
.Cm status | load | unload | allow | deny | reset | enable | disable
.Ar module
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
contacts the
.Dq system.SystemExtension
capability service using the caller's discovery authority.
All changes to policy are validated and persisted by the service.
The client does not edit configuration files or invoke
.Xr kldload 2 .
.Pp
The inspection commands
.Pq Cm status , list , config
use the full service when the caller reaches it, and otherwise its
read-only view,
.Dq system.SystemExtension.Status .
.Xr switchboard 8
resolves the read-only view for a process with effective UID 0, outside any
jail, that holds no software grant: a root login shell, a cron job, or a
command started from
.Pa /etc/rc.local
after
.Pa /etc/rc
exits
.Pq Pa /Capabilities/Config/switchboard/root-query.ucl .
The view has no operation that loads a module or changes policy.
For the same callers,
.Xr switchboard 8
also resolves
.Dq system.SystemExtension.RootLoad
.Pq Pa /Capabilities/Config/switchboard/root-load.ucl ,
which has two operations,
.Cm load
and
.Cm unload ,
limited to modules of installed 5BSD base packages; see
.Sx ROOT LOADS .
Other users reach none of these endpoints and get nothing.
The load and policy commands never use the read-only view, and the policy
commands never use the root-load endpoint.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width disable
.It Cm status
Print what the kernel and the broker report, read-only:
the modules compiled into the kernel, the module files loaded since boot
(from
.Xr kldstat 2
and
.Xr modstat 2 ) ,
and the broker's recent load decisions, oldest first.
Each decision names the module, the requesting switchboard client label,
and the outcome: loaded, already loaded, or the reason it was refused
.Po
not on the allow-list and not a hardware driver of this kernel;
client may load hardware drivers only; denied by an administrator override;
or the load failed with an error
.Pc .
Root loads and unloads appear as
.Dq root load
and
.Dq root unload
with the client
.Dq root (uid 0)
and their own refusal reasons
.Pq see Sx ROOT LOADS .
The broker keeps the 64 most recent decisions in memory since it started.
Requests that never reach the broker, such as a direct
.Xr kldload 8
refused by the module-loading gate or a
.Nm Cm load
from a shell without a grant, are not decisions and are not listed.
This command needs the read-only view and fails with
.Dv EX_UNAVAILABLE
for any other caller.
.It Cm list
Print permitted module names and their current state,
.Dq loaded
or
.Dq allowed ,
using bounded, lexical discovery.
An entry marked allowed is permitted but not currently loaded.
Each entry also includes an
.Dq installed
field: yes, no, or unknown.
This checks for a regular file named after the entry, with an optional
.Pa .ko
suffix, in the directories specified by
.Va kern.module_path
as visible to the caller.
An inaccessible search path or failed query yields unknown unless a file is found.
This is a file-presence check; it does not resolve module aliases through
.Pa linker.hints
or verify dependencies, compatibility, or package registration.
Each returned entry is coherent; the entire enumeration is not a snapshot
across concurrent edits.
.It Cm config
Show permission, desired boot activation, current loaded state, installed file
presence, and whether
an administrator override exists for each known policy entry.
This is a policy listing, not a catalogue of installed packages.
Loaded state is not disclosed for denied modules.
.It Cm status Ar module
Show the same information for one module.
Over the read-only view, the broker's recent load decisions for that module
follow.
.It Cm load Ar module
Ensure a permitted module is loaded for this boot.
An already loaded module is success.
Only a caller holding a software grant for the service reaches it:
.Pa /etc/rc
and its descendants while
.Pa /etc/rc
runs, and, after boot,
.Xr devd 8
and its actions
.Pq hardware drivers only; see Xr BSDExtension 8 .
A login shell has no such grant.
As root, outside a jail,
.Cm load
then uses the root-load endpoint instead
.Pq see Sx ROOT LOADS ;
for other users the service is unavailable
.Pq Dv EX_UNAVAILABLE .
A module name, never a path, is accepted; a name containing
.Ql /
is refused with
.Dv EX_USAGE .
.It Cm unload Ar module
Unload a module that
.Cm load
loaded through the root-load endpoint since the broker started, from a root
login shell, including a dependency that load brought in (unload the module
that needs it first).
Nothing else is unloaded: not a module that rc, devd or another client
loaded or has asked for since, not a module compiled into the kernel, and
never a protected module
.Pq see Sx ROOT LOADS .
The kernel still refuses a module that is in use, and keeps one that another
loaded module depends on: both are reported as still loaded
.Pq Er EBUSY ,
and the module can be unloaded once nothing needs it.
.It Cm allow Ar module
Persist an administrator permission to load this module.
Permission does not install, authenticate, or load module code.
.It Cm deny Ar module
Persist a denial of future load requests and restoration.
An existing desired boot activation is retained but reported as blocked.
.It Cm reset Ar module
Remove the entire administrator override, including desired activation,
returning permission to the shipped policy.
.It Cm enable Ar module
Persist desired boot activation of an already permitted module.
Use
.Cm load
for immediate activation, or
.Cm restore
to apply all enabled entries.
.It Cm disable Ar module
Remove desired boot activation while retaining any permission override.
This does not forcibly unload shared kernel code.
.It Cm reload
Reread the service's shipped configuration through its Config descriptor.
Administrator overrides are preserved.
Malformed replacements leave the previous policy intact.
.It Cm restore
Load permitted entries with desired boot activation.
The late
.Pa /etc/rc.d/kld
service invokes this after local filesystems mount, even with an empty
.Va kld_list .
.El
.Pp
.Cm allow , deny , reset , enable , disable , reload ,
and
.Cm restore
require ADMIN rights on the service channel, not merely UID 0.
Persistent overrides live in
.Pa /etc/bsdextension/overrides.ucl ,
in a private directory delivered to the service by descriptor.
They survive package updates.
The service validates and syncs a replacement before publishing it to clients.
Requests already admitted under an earlier policy may finish.
.Pp
The administrator directory is on the root filesystem and is read before
serving requests, including bootstrap loads.
It does not depend on the ZFS-backed filesystem service, which may itself
need an extension to start.
This supports UFS installations and read-only installer media; edits on
read-only media fail without changing the active policy.
Missing or corrupt administrator state fails closed, except that an absent
overrides file in the delivered directory means no overrides.
.Pp
There is no force-unload command.
Disabling activation or denying permission leaves existing kernel code loaded
until reboot or normal ownership reclamation permits removal.
Session and boot loads carry a boot-long ownership claim.
.Sh ROOT LOADS
A root login shell has no software grant, and
.Xr kldload 8
from it stays refused by the module-loading gate.
It can still load a kernel module that a 5BSD base package installed:
.Nm Cm load Ar module
from such a shell asks the broker, over
.Dq system.SystemExtension.RootLoad ,
to load
.Ar module Ns Pa .ko
from the running kernel's module directory, the one it was booted from,
normally
.Pa /boot/kernel .
It never loads a protected module: a security policy
.Pq Pa mac_* ,
a tracing provider
.Pq DTrace and its providers, HyperTrace, kinst, hwpmc, ksyms ,
ZFS and opensolaris, a packet filter
.Pq pf, pflog, pfsync, pflow, ipfw and its modules, dummynet, ipl ,
or vmm; the same modules
.Cm unload
refuses, refused with
.Dq protected module .
Load them through
.Xr loader.conf 5
or
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
Before anything is loaded the broker works out every module file the load
would bring in for the module's dependencies
.Pq Dv MODULE_DEPEND ,
from the files' module metadata and the kernel directory's
.Pa linker.hints ,
as the kernel would; it loads them first, each by its absolute path, then the
module, and only when every one of these files passes all of these checks:
.Bl -bullet
.It
It is a regular file in the running kernel's directory, not a symbolic link;
files in
.Pa /boot/modules
or elsewhere are refused, and so is a module that needs one.
The directory must still hold the running kernel: after
.Cm installkernel
or a kernel package upgrade, root loads wait for the reboot.
So they do after
.Pa /boot
itself was renamed and replaced, or mounted over.
.It
The local package database
.Pq Pa /var/db/pkg/local.sqlite
records that path in a package whose origin is
.Pa base/...
and that was installed from the repository the broker's policy names
.Po
.Va root_load_repository
in
.Pa bsdextension.ucl ;
shipped:
.Dq 5BSD-base
.Pc .
.It
The file's current content matches the checksum
.Xr pkg 8
recorded at installation
.Po
pkg records a BLAKE2b-512 checksum, not SHA-256; the broker computes the same
.Pc .
.It
No administrator override denies it
.Pq Nm Cm deny ,
and it is not a protected module.
.It
It provides the module version the file that needs it declares.
A dependency that is already loaded must be the kernel's own or the file the
kernel directory's
.Pa linker.hints
names for the version range; a module of that name loaded from another file
has a version the broker cannot check, and the load is refused.
.It
It did not change while it was checked and loaded, the absolute path the
kernel loaded still names it, and nothing else was loaded meanwhile;
otherwise everything the request loaded is unloaded again, and nothing it did
not load.
.El
.Pp
Each refusal has its own message and exit status
.Dv EX_NOPERM :
not a file of an installed 5BSD base package; the file does not match the
checksum its package recorded; no regular module file of that name in the
kernel directory, a dependency outside it, or a kernel directory that no
longer holds the running kernel; denied by an administrator override; root
loads are not configured; the package database could not be read; a file
changed or an unchecked file was loaded during the load; protected module.
A dependency loaded from another file, whose version cannot be checked, is
refused with
.Dv EX_OSERR .
A load the kernel itself fails exits with
.Dv EX_OSERR .
Every request, granted or refused, is kept in the broker's decision list
.Pq Nm Cm status ,
logged by the broker with the reason, and logged by
.Nm
to the
.Dq auth
facility with the login name, terminal, and the audit user and session
identifiers when the kernel has audit support.
The broker's own record carries the switchboard label and the program
instance identifier; the kernel stamp carries no login session.
.Pp
What this protects against: a root login shell loading an arbitrary file,
a module copied into
.Pa /boot/kernel
by hand, a module modified after installation, or a third-party module, by
mistake or by a careless script; and it leaves a record of every attempt.
What it does not protect against: a root that is itself compromised.
Root can install or replace packages, edit the package database, replace a
file between the check and the load, replace or mount over
.Pa /boot ,
or add the module to
.Xr loader.conf 5
and reboot.
The package database and
.Pa /boot
are writable by root, so they are not a trust anchor.
.Xr mac_veriexec 4
fingerprint verification is the defence against a compromised root.
.Pp
Who counts as root: the endpoint is resolved from the kernel-stamped
.Em effective
UID; the stamp carries no real UID, so a set-user-ID-root program run by
another user is root here, as it is for
.Cm status .
Any root process without a software grant qualifies, not only a login shell:
a cron job, or
.Xr devd 8
restarted from a root shell
.Pq it then runs without its lasting grant ,
can load any base module that passes these checks.
The hardware-driver limit on devd's own grant does not bound that.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/SystemExtension.cap/Units/bsdextension.unit/Config/bsdextension.ucl
Shipped defaults; the allowed_extensions array replaces built-in defaults.
.It Pa /etc/bsdextension/overrides.ucl
Versioned administrator state.
Manage it through this command rather than editing storage internals.
.It Pa /Capabilities/Config/switchboard/root-query.ucl
Read-only endpoints switchboard resolves for UID 0 sessions without a grant.
.It Pa /Capabilities/Config/switchboard/root-load.ucl
The root-load endpoint switchboard resolves for the same sessions.
.It Pa /var/db/pkg/local.sqlite
The package database root loads check, read by the broker.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success, including a root load of a module that is already
loaded.
A refused root load or unload exits with
.Dv EX_NOPERM .
.Cm status
returns 1 for a denied or unloaded module.
Persistent operations return
.Dv EX_TEMPFAIL
if administrator configuration has not been attached.
Other errors use
.Xr sysexits 3
codes.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Why did a driver not load, from a root login shell:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysextctl status if_axge
if_axge: allowed, boot=disabled, loaded=yes, installed=yes, policy=default
  2026-11-01 05:12:09 load if_axge (client devd): loaded
# sysextctl status
.Ed
.Pp
Load a base system module from a root login shell, and remove it again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysextctl load ext2fs
ext2fs: loaded
# sysextctl unload ext2fs
ext2fs: unloaded
# cp /tmp/x.ko /boot/kernel/x.ko; sysextctl load x
sysextctl: x: refused: not a file of an installed 5BSD base package
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mac_veriexec 4 ,
.Xr BSDExtension 8 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr pkg 8 ,
.Xr switchboard 8
